/**
 * @page theory Theory
 * @brief Theoretical Background

 * This page provides a detailed overview of the theoretical framework behind the atomistic stress tensor calculations.

 * ## Hardy Cauchy Stress Tensor

 * The Hardy Cauchy stress is defined as:

 * \f[
 * \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x},t) =
 *      -\sum_i m_i \mathbf{v}^{\,i}_{rel}(\mathbf{x},t)
 *      \otimes \mathbf{v}^{\,i}_{rel}(\mathbf{x},t)
 *      w(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^i(t))
 *      - \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j \ne i} \mathbf{f}_{ij} \otimes  (\mathbf{x}^i-\mathbf{x}^j) b(\mathbf x; \mathbf x^i, \mathbf x^j)
 * \f]

 * where \f$ w(\cdot) \f$ is a normalized weighting function and \f$b\f$ is
 * the associated bond function.  The kinetic part uses the relative velocity
 * \f[
 * \mathbf{v}^{\,i}_{rel}(\mathbf{x},t)=\mathbf{v}^i(t)-\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t),
 * \f]
 * with continuum momentum density and mass density
 * \f[
 * \mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x},t)=\sum_i m_i\mathbf{v}^i(t)w(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^i(t)),
 * \qquad
 * \rho(\mathbf{x},t)=\sum_i m_i w(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^i(t)).
 * \f]
 * The continuum velocity is
 * \f[
 * \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t)=
 * \begin{cases}
 * \mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x},t)/\rho(\mathbf{x},t), & \rho(\mathbf{x},t)>0,\\
 * \mathbf{0}, & \rho(\mathbf{x},t)=0.
 * \end{cases}
 * \f]
 *
 * MDStressLab writes \f$\mathbf p\f$ and \f$\rho\f$ for Cauchy stresses in
 * separate `.momentum_density` and `.mass_density` files.  These fields are
 * not defined for Piola stress.

 * ## Piola Stress

 * The Piola stress implemented in MDStressLab contains only the potential
 * contribution.  Its kinetic contribution is zero.

 * ## Hardy and Virial Stresses

 * 

 * ## Lattice Dependent Averaging Domains

 * LDAD methods use a separable pointwise weight in the basis of the LDAD
 * vectors.  Direct pointwise evaluations of this weight, used by kinetic
 * Cauchy stress and density fields, are normalized to have unit integral.
 * The LDAD bond function used by potential stress uses its own line-integral
 * normalization and remains independent of the pointwise operator.

 * ## Additional Topics

 * - See @ref visualization_intro for visualizing stress fields.
 * - See the `Stress` class for implementation details of the output stress fields.
 */
